ual, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "15 – 16 October 1968"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/488"} "Sala de Armas Fernando Montes de Oca, Ciudad Deportiva Magdalena Mixhuca, Ciudad de México"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/93474"} "64 from 25 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The Soviet Union had provided the last two World Champions in " [:a {:href "/athletes/23594"} "Viktor Putiatin"] "and " [:a {:href "/athletes/23609"} "German Sveshnikov"] " so it came as a surprise that none of their fencers qualified for the final six man pool to decide the medal positions.  In contrast France, led by former World Champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/21947"} "Jean-Claude Magnan"] ", qualified their entire team for the final stages and were joined by two Romanians and a Hungarian.  One of the Romanians was " [:a {:href "/athletes/23213"} "Ion Drîmbă"] ", a member of the reigning World Champion team but someone who had never won an individual World or Olympic medal despite a decade of trying. Drîmbă lost one of his five matches in the final pool but it was still enough to claim an outright win."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The only other two fencers with a winning record, " [:a {:href "/athletes/22473"} "Jenő Kamuti"] " of Hungary and 1964 bronze medallist " [:a {:href "/athletes/22021"} "Daniel Revenu"] " of France, fought off for silver with the Hungarian emerging triumphant."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The champion won a bronze medal with the Romanian team at the 1969 World Championships but then defected to the West. He lived in Germany and the USA before settling permanently in Brazil."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "20150"